# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Container image for apps/web (the Next.js client).
#
# Read the header of Dockerfile.api first: the base-image digest, the reason
# there is no `# syntax=` directive, the stage layout, the "two installs beat
# install-then-prune" decision, the reason the manifests are NOT copied one by one
# (`verifyDepsBeforeRun: error` turns a stale COPY list into a hard build failure
# the day somebody adds a package), and the symlink-farm constraint that forces
# every stage to live at /app are all explained there and all apply identically
# here. This header only covers what is different about the web image.
#
# ## The base image digest
#
# Same image, same digest, same refresh command as Dockerfile.api. When you
# refresh one, refresh both — an api and a web container built on different
# base-OS patch levels is a debugging trap for whoever is on call.
#
#     node:24.18.0-alpine3.24
#     sha256:a0b9bf06e4e6193cf7a0f58816cc935ff8c2a908f81e6f1a95432d679c54fbfd
#     multi-arch OCI index (linux/amd64, linux/arm64/v8, linux/s390x)
#     captured 2026-08-25
#
# ## Why Alpine
#
# This file said `node:24.18.0-bookworm-slim@sha256:6f7b03f7…51452d` until
# 2026-08-25. Dockerfile.api's header carries the full argument and the trivy
# table; the short version is that the runtime stage descends from `base`, so the
# shipped image's OS package set IS the base's, and that set was 88 packages
# carrying every HIGH/CRITICAL finding this project shipped. Alpine's is 18 and
# scans at zero. Two things specific to THIS image made it safe to do:
#
#   * `@next/swc-linux-x64-musl` and `@next/swc-linux-arm64-musl` are already in
#     pnpm-lock.yaml, as are `@img/sharp-linuxmusl-{x64,arm64}`. Next's compiler
#     and sharp are the two native binaries in this tree, and pnpm picks the musl
#     builds by install platform — there is no `supportedArchitectures` block
#     forcing a libc, so `--frozen-lockfile` is still satisfied on Alpine.
#   * Nothing is compiled from source. `ignoreScripts: true` in
#     pnpm-workspace.yaml plus `--ignore-scripts` on both installs means node-gyp
#     never runs, so there is no glibc-linked addon to rebuild.
#
# The honest caveat, the same one as in Dockerfile.api: musl is not glibc. This
# image builds, scans clean and boots; a full staging run is what actually
# settles it, and that has not happened at the time of writing.
#
# There is no bash on Alpine — `/bin/sh` is busybox ash, which is what Docker's
# default `SHELL` already invoked. The `for pkg in packages/*/` loop in the build
# stage below is POSIX and stays that way. Keep any new `RUN` POSIX too.
#
# ## Why the runtime runs `next start` through the workspace bin shim
#
# `next start` needs the `next` package, which is a production dependency, so it
# is present in the prod-deps tree. It is invoked as
# /app/apps/web/node_modules/.bin/next — pnpm writes a small executable wrapper
# there that resolves the real entry point. Hard-coding the path inside
# node_modules/.pnpm instead would embed the peer-dependency hash pnpm appends to
# that directory name (`next@16.3.0_@types+node@…_react@…`), which changes
# whenever a peer moves. The shim is the stable name.
#
# Note what this is NOT: `pnpm start`. Running pnpm at container start would fire
# `verifyDepsBeforeRun: error` (pnpm-workspace.yaml), re-verifying the whole
# dependency tree against the lockfile on every boot — slow, and guaranteed to
# fail on an image that has deliberately had its sources removed.
#
# ## If the web app ever sets `output: 'standalone'`
#
# Next's standalone build emits `.next/standalone/server.js` with a minimal,
# self-contained node_modules and is the better shape for a container. It has to
# be enabled in the web app's own next.config, which this file does not own. If
# and when that lands, replace the runtime stage's CMD with
# `["node", "apps/web/.next/standalone/apps/web/server.js"]`, copy
# `.next/static` and `public` into the standalone tree as Next's docs describe,
# and delete the prod-deps copy from this stage — it stops being needed.
#
# ## The runtime working directory is the web app, not the repo root
#
# `next start` has no `--dir` in its argv here, so it resolves BOTH `next.config.*`
# and `.next/` relative to the process working directory. The base stage sets
# `WORKDIR /app` (the repo root, which is where the symlink farm has to live for the
# builds), and a runtime stage that inherits it looks for `/app/.next` — which does
# not exist, because the build output is at `/app/apps/web/.next`. The container then
# exits 1 with "Could not find a production build in the '.next' directory" and
# crash-loops under compose's `restart: unless-stopped`; the image itself is fine.
#
# The tell, if this ever regresses: the boot log says "Running next.config took Nms"
# instead of "Running next.config.ts took Nms". From the wrong directory Next finds no
# config file either, and reports the generic name.
#
# So the runtime stage sets `WORKDIR /app/apps/web` explicitly. It is set AFTER the
# COPYs on purpose — every COPY destination below is absolute, but a reader should not
# have to check that to know the copies are unaffected. `working_dir:` in
# docker-compose.yml would fix the compose path only, and leave `docker run` broken;
# this belongs to the image.
#
# ## Writable paths
#
# `next start` writes to `.next/cache` (ISR revalidation, the image optimiser).
# The build cache is stripped from the image on purpose — it is build-time state,
# not runtime state, and it is large. With a read-only root filesystem the
# directory has to come back as a tmpfs; docker-compose.yml mounts one at
# /app/apps/web/.next/cache and explains why. Without that mount, a read-only
# container will serve pages but log EROFS on the first revalidation.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

FROM node:24.18.0-alpine3.24@sha256:a0b9bf06e4e6193cf7a0f58816cc935ff8c2a908f81e6f1a95432d679c54fbfd AS base
WORKDIR /app

# OpenSSL, ahead of the base image.
#
# CVE-2026-14456, HIGH, in libcrypto3/libssl3 3.5.7-r0 — which is what this
# pinned digest ships. `security:audit` blocked the release on it: 4 findings, 0
# unfixable, across both images. It is not a false positive and it is not
# waivable; it is the TLS library the API terminates nothing with but links
# against, in an image about to serve students.
#
# Upstream has not rebuilt the tag. Measured on 2026-08-26: the pinned digest is
# still what `node:24.18.0-alpine3.24` resolves to, and `node:24-alpine`
# (v24.19.0) ships the same 3.5.7-r0. So there is no newer base to move to, and
# waiting is not a plan when the fix is sitting in the repository the image
# already trusts — `apk policy` reports 3.5.8-r0 in alpine/v3.24/main.
#
# Pinned to the exact version rather than a bare `apk upgrade`. An unpinned
# upgrade makes two builds of the same commit differ, which is the property
# `release-artifacts.mjs` exists to protect. The cost is that this line FAILS the
# build when Alpine drops 3.5.8-r0 for a later fix — and that is the safe
# direction: a build that stops and names the package is a bump somebody makes on
# purpose, where a silently-floating one is a version nobody chose.
#
# In `base`, so every stage derived from it — toolchain, build and runtime —
# carries the fix. The runtime filesystem is read-only and the process is uid
# 1000, so nothing can undo it later.
RUN apk add --no-cache --upgrade libcrypto3=3.5.8-r0 libssl3=3.5.8-r0

FROM base AS toolchain
ENV COREPACK_ENABLE_DOWNLOAD_PROMPT=0 \
    NPM_CONFIG_UPDATE_NOTIFIER=false
RUN corepack enable

# .dockerignore keeps node_modules, dist, .next, coverage, .git, deploy, docs,
# markdown and .env* out of the context, so this is source and manifests only. The
# markdown and .env rules are anchored at every depth; before they were, this
# image shipped apps/web/AGENTS.md and apps/web/CLAUDE.md.
FROM toolchain AS source
COPY . .

# `--filter "@prl/web..."` is the web app plus its workspace dependencies, so
# Fastify, @fastify/* and postgres never enter this image.
FROM source AS prod-deps
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --prod --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/web..."
# A deny list, as in Dockerfile.api: worst case a fat image, never a broken one.
# `packages/ui/src` goes here with everything else and is put back from the build
# stage below — @prl/ui is consumed as source, so keeping the prune uniform and
# restoring the one exception is better than a hand-maintained package list.
# See the identical markdown lines in Dockerfile.api: .dockerignore is the real
# fix and these are the second line of defence, because THIS image is the one that
# shipped apps/web/AGENTS.md and apps/web/CLAUDE.md.
# `tsconfig*.json`, not `tsconfig.json`: the narrow spelling matched only the base
# config and left every `tsconfig.test.json` behind. Verified in the released
# image — prl-web shipped apps/api/tsconfig.test.json and five
# packages/*/tsconfig.test.json under a .dockerignore that says the test trees and
# tsconfigs "are removed inside the images by the prune stages".
# `docker-compose*.yml`, not the two names it used to spell out: there is a third
# compose file and prl-web@sha256:6a63e44f shipped it — see the same paragraph in
# Dockerfile.api for the measurement. `.gitignore` and `.dockerignore` go with
# them; nothing at runtime reads either, and .gitignore is a list of where this
# deployment's secrets live.
RUN rm -rf \
      apps/*/src apps/*/test apps/*/scripts \
      packages/*/src packages/*/test \
      apps/*/tsconfig*.json packages/*/tsconfig*.json tsconfig.base.json \
      biome.json db .github scripts Dockerfile.api Dockerfile.web \
      docker-compose*.yml .dockerignore .gitignore deploy \
      ./*.md apps/*/*.md packages/*/*.md

FROM source AS build
RUN pnpm install --frozen-lockfile --ignore-scripts --filter "@prl/web..."

# The `/api/*` rewrite destination, and the ONLY build-time setting in this image.
#
# `next build` writes the resolved destination into .next/routes-manifest.json and
# `next start` reads the manifest rather than re-running next.config.ts, so this is
# genuinely frozen at build time — which is why it has its own name instead of reusing
# PRL_API_ORIGIN, the variable the running server reads for its own fetches. One name
# per lifetime; the last design gave one name two lifetimes and that is the defect
# being repaired here.
#
# Leave it alone for a real deployment. A deployment routes /api/* at the proxy in
# front of this container (docker-compose.yml, docs/RUNBOOK.md §11) and never reaches
# the rewrite. Set it only for the case it exists for: `docker run` of this image on a
# laptop whose API is somewhere other than localhost:3001, with no proxy in play.
#
#     docker build -f Dockerfile.web --build-arg PRL_API_REWRITE_ORIGIN=http://host.docker.internal:3001 .
#
# It is an origin, not a credential, so a value here in `docker history` is not a leak
# — and next.config.ts refuses to start a production server if anyone tries to set it
# as a runtime environment variable, because there it would do nothing at all.
ARG PRL_API_REWRITE_ORIGIN=http://localhost:3001
ENV PRL_API_REWRITE_ORIGIN=${PRL_API_REWRITE_ORIGIN}

# Topological: the workspace packages emit their dist/*.d.ts, packages/ui
# typechecks, then `next build` runs. @prl/ui is consumed as source and
# transpiled by Next, which is why ui has no dist to copy later.
RUN pnpm --filter "@prl/web..." run build
# Reduce this stage to what the runtime should lay over prod-deps. Sources and
# tsconfigs are not read by `next start`; .next/cache is build cache. Both
# node_modules trees MUST go: this stage holds a FULL install, and letting it land on
# top of prod-deps' would silently reinstate every devDependency in the shipped image.
#
# packages/ui/src is the one source tree that stays. @prl/ui resolves its `.` export
# straight to src/index.ts, so the directory has to exist for the symlink in
# apps/web/node_modules/@prl/ui to resolve if anything reads it at runtime.
#
# Pruning here, then copying /app/packages as one directory below, replaces a
# hand-written per-package list of COPY lines. That list is a latent boot failure: the
# day the app gains a dependency on a workspace package nobody adds to it, the image
# builds cleanly and dies at startup with ERR_MODULE_NOT_FOUND. That is not
# hypothetical — it happened to Dockerfile.api with @prl/poker-ai.
# `apps/web/*.md` is the line that matters most in this file. The runtime stage
# copies /app/apps/web as one directory, so anything left here is published; that
# is exactly how AGENTS.md and CLAUDE.md got into the image.
#
# `apps/web/test` is on this line for the same reason, and it was missing. The
# prod-deps prune above did remove it, but the runtime stage lays THIS stage's
# /app/apps/web over that already-pruned tree — so a directory pruned once at the
# bottom and not at the top comes straight back. Measured in the released image:
# prl-web@sha256:2bc457c…31a8bd88 shipped /app/apps/web/test, 26 files and 320 KB
# of tests, contradicting .dockerignore's claim that the test trees are "removed
# inside the images by the prune stages". Anything pruned in prod-deps that lives
# under apps/web has to be pruned here too or the copy undoes it.
#
# `tsconfig*.json` rather than `tsconfig.json`, here and for packages: the narrow
# spelling never matched `tsconfig.test.json`, and those shipped too.
RUN rm -rf apps/web/node_modules apps/web/src apps/web/test apps/web/tsconfig*.json apps/web/.next/cache \
      apps/web/*.md packages/*/*.md \
      packages/*/node_modules packages/*/test packages/*/tsconfig*.json \
 && for pkg in packages/*/; do [ "$pkg" = 'packages/ui/' ] || rm -rf "${pkg}src"; done

FROM base AS runtime

# HOSTNAME matters: Next binds to localhost by default in some configurations,
# and a server listening on 127.0.0.1 inside a container is unreachable from
# outside it. 0.0.0.0 is correct here precisely because the container's network
# namespace is the isolation boundary.
ENV NODE_ENV=production \
    PORT=3000 \
    HOSTNAME=0.0.0.0

# npm, corepack and yarn are removed for the reasons written out at length in the
# same place in Dockerfile.api: measured, npm's bundled tree was once the source
# of every HIGH/CRITICAL finding in this image that had an available fix (all
# inside /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm); structurally, a package manager next to
# a production application is a downloader and an arbitrary-code executor in a
# container whose whole posture is a read-only root and a non-root user.
#
# The three yarn paths arrived with the Alpine base: bookworm-slim shipped no
# yarn, `node:24.18.0-alpine3.24` ships yarn 1.22.22 at /opt/yarn-v1.22.22 with
# /usr/local/bin/yarn and /usr/local/bin/yarnpkg pointing at it. Leaving them
# would have put a second package manager back into an image that claims to have
# none. This is a deny list: re-check `ls /usr/local/bin /opt` whenever the base
# changes.
#
# The CMD below is /app/apps/web/node_modules/.bin/next, a pnpm-written shim that
# execs node directly. It does not go near npm or yarn.
RUN rm -rf /usr/local/lib/node_modules/npm /usr/local/lib/node_modules/corepack \
      /usr/local/bin/npm /usr/local/bin/npx /usr/local/bin/corepack \
      /opt/yarn-v1.22.22 /usr/local/bin/yarn /usr/local/bin/yarnpkg

COPY --from=prod-deps --chown=node:node /app /app
# Every workspace package's dist/ — whichever they are today — plus
# packages/content/data and packages/ui/src. COPY merges into an existing directory,
# so prod-deps' node_modules symlink farm survives underneath. See the prune above for
# why this is one line and not one line per package.
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/packages /app/packages
# Everything the built web app needs: .next, public, next.config.*, package.json.
# Copied as one directory because the exact set (does public/ exist? is the
# config .ts, .mjs or .js?) is the web app's business, not this file's — and a
# COPY of a glob that matches nothing is a build failure, not a no-op.
COPY --from=build --chown=node:node /app/apps/web /app/apps/web

# `next start` resolves next.config.* and .next/ from here. See the header.
WORKDIR /app/apps/web

USER node

EXPOSE 3000

# `/` rather than a dedicated health route: the web app is a page server, and
# "the home page renders" is the honest statement of its health. See
# Dockerfile.api for why the probe is `node -e` and not curl, and why it targets
# `os.hostname()` — the container's address on the container network — rather than
# 127.0.0.1. Note that `os.hostname()` reads the kernel hostname and is therefore
# unaffected by the HOSTNAME variable above, which is Next's bind address.
HEALTHCHECK --interval=30s --timeout=5s --start-period=30s --retries=3 \
  CMD ["node", "-e", "fetch(`http://${require('node:os').hostname()}:${process.env.PORT ?? 3000}/`).then((r)=>process.exit(r.ok?0:1)).catch(()=>process.exit(1))"]

CMD ["/app/apps/web/node_modules/.bin/next", "start"]
